A||||6||GT-AutoX||real time diagnostic mode||||5||
Z||000000||GT-AutoX||06-05-2001||10:12 AM||||Isn't there some sort of mode where you can get the check engine light to flash a rich or lean running condition while driving?  How do you set this up, and how do you read the flashes?||||reg||5
Z||000001||84Bill||06-05-2001||10:22 AM||||Looks like you will need a Scan tool <BR>Here is an excelent site to check out.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.techedge.com.au/vehicle/aldl8192/8192hw.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.techedge.com.au/vehicle/aldl8192/8192hw.htm</A> <P><BR>Early ECUs produced a fixed 8192 ALDL data stream when the user placed a 10k ohm resistor between ALDL connector pins A and B. Later ECUs added internal receive circuitry<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-05-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-05-2001||11:26 AM||||I don think you need the scan tool to use the check engine light indicator. You jumper the AB pins on the test connector behind the lighter panel (same ones to spill the codes) after the engine is running and it will blink once a second when the engine is running open loop, and 3 times a second when running closed loop - and the light will pulse on longer if its running rich, and shorter if its running lean.<P>  do remember the engine runs open loop until its warmed up - so dont freak out for the first few minutes.<P>  if you hook up the scan tool you can get the acutal numbers from the ECM as you are driving - but the check engine light by itself will at least give you some basic info.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroLT1||06-05-2001||03:15 PM||||Just for such ocassions I use the Auto X-Ray EZ-Link GM OBD-I Scanner. Besides scanning all codes and telling you in plain English what is wrong, is has an On-demand Capture mode, which records sensor values up to 29 seconds when you need it to diagnose intermittent problems on the run.<P>Good Luck,<BR><P>------------------<BR>Winston Ojeda<BR><B>88 Formula LT1</B><BR>Milwaukee, Wisconsin, USA<BR><A HREF="http://www.FieroLT1.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.FieroLT1.com</A><BR><I>Home of the Fiero LT1</I><BR>  <A HREF="mailto:wojeda@FieroLT1.com">wojeda@FieroLT1.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||06-05-2001||07:18 PM||||Ken, we're doing real time work here... not checking codes. The ECM will report sensor and other data in real time but you need a scanner.<P>I use the AutoXray one myself. There are various PC ones around as well.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.autoxray.com" TARGET=_blank>www.autoxray.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Pontiaddict||06-06-2001||02:04 AM||||Ken, if you jump the terminals on the aldl and run the engine, the computer pulls the timing put of the motor. (that's why you do that to check the distributor timing) No advance is something that makes for fun driving. I accidentally did something similar on a firebird. I wouldn't recommend driving around like that.||||reg||1
Z||000006||84Bill||06-06-2001||07:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B><BR>I use the AutoXray one myself. There are various PC ones around as well.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.autoxray.com" TARGET=_blank>www.autoxray.com</A> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Hey Ogre<BR>How do you like this scantool?<BR>I'm looking to get away from this hoakey DIY stuff.<BR>||||reg||14
